{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "12-limits.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "What CRDTs cannot do",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find the boundary of the promise: replicas that converge agree, but what they agree on can still be wrong, and some invariants cannot be kept without coordination.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A bank balance as a PN-Counter, with one rule: it must never go below zero. The balance is 1, and both replicas know it. Each receives a withdrawal of 1 and approves it, because locally the balance is 1.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Predict the balance once the replicas have synced.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "replicas(pncounter; a, b\n  a: inc\n  a -> b\n  a: dec\n  b: dec\n  a -> b\n  b -> a\n)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Both read $-1$. They have converged, and the rule is broken. No cleverer merge fixes it: in the Lean, `no_bank_merge` shows that no idempotent merge can honour both of two withdrawals of 80 from 100. Idempotence forces the merge of $20$ with $20$ to be $20$, while honouring both withdrawals needs $-60$. Each replica was right about its own state and neither could know about the other's withdrawal without asking it first.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same boundary showed up earlier. LWW converges by discarding one of two concurrent writes. Tombstones pile up for ever, and throwing one away safely needs to know that every replica has seen its remove: agreement about the state of everyone, which is coordination again.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Invariants that span replicas (no overdraft, unique usernames, at most ten seats sold) need coordination somewhere: a lock, consensus, or an escrow that hands each replica a share it may spend alone. CRDTs are for the state that does not need it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/- ============================================================\n   Crdt.Limits — Chapter 13: What CRDTs Cannot Do\n   ============================================================ -/\n\nnamespace Limits\n\n/-- Refuted: \"there is a merge for non-negative bank accounts\".\n    Suppose a merge that is duplicate-safe (idempotent) and honors\n    both replicas' withdrawals from a shared initial balance of 100.\n    Two concurrent withdrawals of 80 force `merge 20 20` to be both\n    `20` (idempotence) and `-60` (honoring both). No such function\n    exists — availability under partition cannot preserve \"balance\n    never goes negative\" while accepting every withdrawal. -/\ntheorem no_bank_merge :\n    ¬ ∃ merge : Int → Int → Int,\n        (∀ a, merge a a = a) ∧\n        (∀ x y : Nat, x ≤ 100 → y ≤ 100 →\n          merge (100 - (x : Int)) (100 - (y : Int))\n            = 100 - (x : Int) - (y : Int)) := by\n  intro ⟨merge, hidem, hboth⟩\n  have h₂ := hboth 80 80 (by omega) (by omega)\n  have he : (100 : Int) - (80 : Nat) = 20 := by decide\n  rw [he, hidem 20] at h₂\n  exact absurd h₂ (by decide)\n\n/-- The PN-Counter happily goes negative: convergence is not\n    correctness. (The Chapter 4 `clampedValue` exercise hid the\n    problem; it did not solve it.) -/\ntheorem pn_goes_negative :\n    PNCounter.value (PNCounter.decr 0 (⊥ : PNCounter 1)) < 0 := by decide\n\nend Limits",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!note] The axiom audit\n> The book closes by printing the axioms every headline theorem depends on. Nothing beyond propositional extensionality and quotient soundness: the whole chain, the main theorem included, is constructive.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/- ============================================================\n   Axiom audit — every headline theorem, `#print axioms`.\n   Result: nothing beyond `propext` (propositional extensionality,\n   used through `funext`/`ext`-style reasoning) and `Quot.sound`\n   (quotients, Chapter 8; also inside core `simp` lemmas about\n   lists). `Classical.choice` appears nowhere: the entire chain,\n   `sec_state_based` included, is constructive.\n   ============================================================ -/\n\n#print axioms Order.sup_comm\n#print axioms Order.sup_assoc\n#print axioms GCounter.value_increment\n#print axioms PNCounter.value_not_monotone\n#print axioms SList.sorted_ext\n#print axioms TotalOrder.maxo_assoc\n#print axioms TwoPSet.no_readd\n#print axioms ORSet.addWins\n#print axioms SEC.foldJoin_eq_of_same_mems\n#print axioms SEC.foldJoin_perm\n#print axioms MSet.foldJoin\n#print axioms SEC.sec_state_based\n#print axioms Delivery.converged_of_allShared\n#print axioms Delivery.gossipUntilQuiescent\n#print axioms Delivery.quiescent_shares\n#print axioms VV.ticks_concurrent\n#print axioms OpBased.opGCounter_simulates\n#print axioms Capstone.sec_store\n#print axioms Limits.no_bank_merge\n\nend Crdt",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> CRDTs buy availability and convergence without coordination, and they cannot buy invariants that relate replicas. Knowing which state needs which is the design decision this course leaves you with.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
